  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe CLOB.sol contract contains logic intended to prevent \"zero-cost\" trades, in which one party might receive assets without returning anything, often due to rounding or dust values.\n\nHowever, the current implementation uses a bitwise AND operation (&) to detect such cases:\n\nhttps://github.com/code-423n4/2025-07-gte-clob/blob/92a8839e3eb9715d53e24f0168442cecdc9b784b/contracts/clob/CLOB.sol#L503-L505\nhttps://github.com/code-423n4/2025-07-gte-clob/blob/92a8839e3eb9715d53e24f0168442cecdc9b784b/contracts/clob/CLOB.sol#L544-L546\n\n```\n// In _processLimitBidOrder \nif (\n    baseTokenAmountReceived != quoteTokenAmountSent &&\n    baseTokenAmountReceived & quoteTokenAmountSent == 0\n) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n\n// In _processLimitAskOrder \nif (\n    baseTokenAmountSent != quoteTokenAmountReceived &&\n    baseTokenAmountSent & quoteTokenAmountReceived == 0\n) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n```\nThis logic is flawed. The bitwise AND between two valid, non-zero values can be zero if they don’t share any set bits (e.g., powers of two like 8 and 16). This leads to false positives, causing legitimate trades to revert.\n\nFor example:\n8 & 16 == 0 despite both being valid, non-zero amounts.\n\nThis misbehavior introduces a Denial of Service (DoS) vector for certain trade sizes, potentially allowing malicious actors or unfortunate users to experience repeated failed transactions.\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nReplace the faulty bitwise check with a proper logical condition that explicitly checks for one-sided zero values:\n\n```\n// Fix in _processLimitBidOrder\nif (\n    (baseTokenAmountReceived > 0 && quoteTokenAmountSent == 0) ||\n    (baseTokenAmountReceived == 0 && quoteTokenAmountSent > 0)\n) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n\n// Fix in _processLimitAskOrder\nif (\n    (baseTokenAmountSent > 0 && quoteTokenAmountReceived == 0) ||\n    (baseTokenAmountSent == 0 && quoteTokenAmountReceived > 0)\n) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "```\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity 0.8.27;\n\nimport \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport {Side} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {\n        address maker = jb;\n        address taker = rite;\n\n        // Maker posts a SELL order\n        uint256 makerSellAmount = 1 ether;\n        uint256 tradePrice = 2 * 1e18;\n\n        vm.startPrank(maker);\n        weth.mint(maker, makerSellAmount);\n        weth.approve(address(accountManager), makerSellAmount);\n        accountManager.deposit(maker, address(weth), makerSellAmount);\n        ICLOB(wethCLOB).postLimitOrder(maker, ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: makerSellAmount,\n            price: tradePrice,\n            cancelTimestamp: 0,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        }));\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Taker attempts to BUY with amounts that falsely trigger ZeroCostTrade\n        uint256 takerBuyAmountBase = 2 ** 53;\n        uint256 takerCostQuote = 2 ** 54;\n\n        assert((takerBuyAmountBase & takerCostQuote) == 0);\n\n        vm.startPrank(taker);\n        tokenA.mint(taker, takerCostQuote);\n        tokenA.approve(address(accountManager), takerCostQuote);\n        accountManager.deposit(taker, address(tokenA), takerCostQuote);\n\n        vm.expectRevert(CLOB.ZeroCostTrade.selector);\n        ICLOB(wethCLOB).postLimitOrder(taker, ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: takerBuyAmountBase,\n            price: tradePrice,\n            cancelTimestamp: 0,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        }));\n        vm.stopPrank();\n    }\n}\n```",
